Sentry's JavaScript Issues product automatically reports errors, uncaught exceptions, and unhandled promise rejections. Initialize it early, then verify the complete path with a deliberate test error from the application itself—not by merely typing an error in DevTools, because browser-console errors are sandboxed and do not trigger Sentry's monitoring. In the Sentry project, check that the issue arrived with the expected environment and release. A useful production setup also records enough context to investigate, while filtering data that should not leave the browser. Test this in a non-production environment or with a clearly labeled test error so verification cannot pollute real incident data.

```ts caption="Initialize the SDK with deployment context and trigger an application-level test error."
import * as Sentry from "@sentry/browser";

Sentry.init({
  dsn: import.meta.env.VITE_SENTRY_DSN,
  environment: import.meta.env.MODE,
  release: import.meta.env.VITE_RELEASE,
});

function verifyMonitoring() {
  throw new Error("observability-test");
}
```

Use structured events with stable field names, a severity that reflects actionability, and deployment context such as environment and release. Log the state needed to explain an operation, not every function call; Sentry recommends a wide event with the relevant context rather than many thin logs. Keep debug-level logging out of production when it is not needed, and redact sensitive attributes before transport. Correlation is more valuable than volume: include identifiers that let an engineer move from a log to its trace, error, or replay. Define ownership and retention separately from the client logging API, because logs can contain high-cardinality user or business data.

```ts caption="Emit one structured operation event with consistent, non-secret attributes."
Sentry.logger.info("Checkout completed", {
  order_id: order.id,
  item_count: order.items.length,
  duration_ms: performance.now() - startedAt,
});
```

RUM, or field data, measures the experience of actual users across their devices, networks, locations, and interactions. Lab tools run controlled simulations and are excellent for development and regression diagnosis, but they cannot represent every real condition. For example, lab tools cannot measure INP without user input, so TBT is used as a lab proxy; field CLS can also include shifts after initial load that a lab page load misses. Track LCP, INP, and CLS in the field and analyze the distribution, not just an average. Google's recommended passing view is the 75th percentile meeting each metric's good threshold: 2.5 seconds, 200 milliseconds, and 0.1 respectively.

```ts caption="Collect field measurements with the web-vitals library and report them to an analytics endpoint."
import { onCLS, onINP, onLCP } from "web-vitals";

const report = (metric: unknown) => {
  navigator.sendBeacon("/analytics", JSON.stringify(metric));
};

onCLS(report);
onINP(report);
onLCP(report);
```

A trace is the end-to-end path of an operation; spans are timed units of work within it. Spans share a trace ID and form a parent-child hierarchy, so a browser operation can be related to an outgoing request, a server handler, a database call, and asynchronous work. Context propagation carries the span context across process boundaries; without it, each service produces disconnected timing data. Instrument boundaries first, use stable operation names and semantic attributes, and attach meaningful events to spans rather than dumping arbitrary payloads. Sample deliberately, especially at high traffic, but preserve enough errors and slow or important business paths to diagnose tail latency. Avoid putting secrets or unbounded, high-cardinality values into attributes without a clear cost and privacy policy.

```ts caption="A span should cover one operation and be ended on every path; exact SDK setup depends on the OpenTelemetry language package."
await tracer.startActiveSpan("checkout", async (span) => {
  try {
    return await createOrder();
  } catch (error) {
    span.recordException(error);
    throw error;
  } finally {
    span.end();
  }
});
```

Treat replay as sensitive telemetry, not as a substitute for error events. Define the allowed data, retention, access, consent, and deletion policy with security and legal stakeholders. Configure masking or blocking for text, inputs, media, and other sensitive surfaces; test those rules with realistic account and checkout flows. Control cost and exposure with sampling: Sentry supports a session sample rate and a separate error sample rate, so ordinary sessions can be sampled while sessions containing errors receive higher priority. Review the performance impact and ad-blocker failure mode. Finally, link replays to errors, traces, logs, and releases so an engineer can move from the symptom to the user journey without exporting a recording.

```ts caption="Illustrative Sentry configuration: sample normal sessions and prioritize sessions with errors; validate masking for your UI."
Sentry.init({
  integrations: [
    Sentry.replayIntegration({ maskAllText: true }),
  ],
  replaysSessionSampleRate: 0.1,
  replaysOnErrorSampleRate: 1.0,
});
```
